Shallow Copy
A shallow copy duplicates only the references of elements at the top level,
meaning that if an element in the array is an object or another array, 
the copy will reference the same nested objects or arrays as the original.
Modifying nested objects in a shallow copy will affect the original array.

Example of a Shallow Copy:

javascript
----
let original = [1, { name: 'John' }];
let shallowCopy = [...original];
---Using spread operator

shallowCopy[1].name = 'Jane'; 
// Modifying nested object
console.log(original[1].name); // 
Output: 'Jane'
In this example, changing shallowCopy[1].name 
also changes original[1].name 
because the inner object { name: 'John' }
is only copied by reference.

Common ways to create a shallow copy in JavaScript:

Using the slice method: array.slice()
Using the spread operator: [...array]
Using Array.from(array)
Deep Copy
A deep copy duplicates all levels of the array,
including any nested arrays or objects.
Modifying a nested element in a deep copy won’t affect the original array.

Example of a Deep Copy:

javascript
let original = [1, { name: 'John' }];
let deepCopy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(original));

deepCopy[1].name = 'Jane';
console.log(original[1].name); 
----Output: 'John'
In this example, changing deepCopy[1].name does not affect original[1].name because all levels have been copied independently.

Common ways to create a deep copy in JavaScript:

Using JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(array)) 
===(only works if the array contains serializable values)
Using libraries like Lodash (_.cloneDeep(array))
